/*****************************************************************************
	This is the tester incantation. It is a working file for testing new features
    under development so it changes when there is a new feature in the works.

    The feature being worked on here is checkSKIP a rewrite in kant as a rule
    to do what the C++ checkSkip() method in GroupRules does that skips space
    on every rule invocation. The register is Grokking so that all the definitions
    are rules.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();

register(Grokking);
define
    EmbeddedComment;
    IndenT=[ \n\t]+;
    BlockCommenT    "/*" EmbeddedCommenT-* commentBody="*/"};
    LineCommenT     "/\/" commentBody="\n"};   // the escape is REQUIRED -- see below
    CommenT
        BlockCommenT;
        LineCommenT;
    commentBrace
        OpenCommenT     "/*";
        CloseCommenT    "*/";
    EmbeddedCommenT     commentBrace{ BlockCommenT;
    sKIP
        DelimitText;
        QuotE;
        IndenT;
        CommenT;
    checkSKIP       sKIP+;;     // second ; ends the define command
    
register(TestStuff);
define
    skipInput=(  "what" /* has /* embedded */ comment*/ NotSkipable#);
    skipTester code={
        print "Did the definnition work?" skipInput:;
        };
    ;
search Grokking TestStuff list;
skipTester();
stop();
/*****************************************************************************
	The following lines are parked here because the next time tester is needed
    they are likely to be needed to be added right after Start(); above
*****************************************************************************/
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Utilities list;

/*****************************************************************************
	The following text is used for testing the checkSKIP rule defined above
*****************************************************************************/

/*****************************************************************************
    WHY LineCommenT IS SPELLED WITH AN ESCAPE  --  Clod, 2026-09-18, measured

    The bare two-character literal KILLS THIS DEFINE. There is no lexer, so the
    parser reads it as a comment inside its own source and eats the rest of the
    line, closing quote and semicolon included. Four runs, one variable each:
    respelling LineCommenT ALONE fixes the file; respelling BlockCommenT ALONE
    does not. The block delimiters were never the problem, and the terminator
    never needed touching. So it is not a Catch-22.

    The ABANDONED report names IndenT, two lines above the offender. Bisect by
    removing later lines, never by staring at the named one.

    The escape is preferred over two adjacent one-character literals, and that
    is a measurement rather than a taste: the escaped form gives ONE term
    matching both characters, the two-literal form gives TWO terms of one
    character each -- and terms are skip points, so a skipper may run between
    them. Pinned as SK-1 and SK-2 in incant/pop/skipT.

    What is NOT yet readable is what the rule CONSUMES. Driven standalone it has
    no enclosing activation to take the label it mints, so its terms still read
    their own definitions afterwards. That is fixIts F-83 and it is station 6.
    docs/checkSKIP.md section 2a carries the whole measurement.
*****************************************************************************/
